\label{thm:feige}
Let $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ be independent random variables with $\mathbb{E} X_i\leq 1$ and $X_i\geq 0$ and let $\delta\geq 1$. Then
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\[
 \mathbb P\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n X_i<n+\delta\Big) \geq \Big(1-\frac{1}{n+\delta}\Big)^n
\]

If the largest fractional matching in $\mathcal{H}$ has weight less than $n/k$, by linear programming duality there is a fractional vertex cover of $\mathcal{H}$ with total weight less than $n/k$. In other words, there are weights $w_v$ for all vertices $v\in V(\mathcal{H})$ such that for each hyperedge $e$ we have $\sum_{v\in e} w_v\geq 1$ and $\sum_{v\in V(\mathcal{H})}w_v<n/k$.

Let $T$ be the set of $d$ vertices with the smallest weight, and note that their average weight $t=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{v\in T}w_v$ is smaller than $1/k$ (since the average weight of all vertices is $<1/k$). Moreover, any vertex $v\notin T$ has $w_v\geq t$. 

Let us now define a nonnegative random variable $X$ as follows: pick a uniformly random vertex $v\in V(\mathcal{H})$ and put $X=0$ if $v\in T$ and $X=\frac{k}{1-kt}(w_v-t)$ if $v\notin T$. To bound the expectation of $X$, observe that
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:expectation_X}
    \mathbb{E}\Big[\frac{1-kt}{k}X\Big]=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{v\in V(\mathcal{H})}(w_v-t)<\frac{1}{n}(n/k-nt)=1/k-t= \frac{1-kt}{k}.\vspace{-0.2cm}
\end{equation}


Note that the contributions to $\mathbb{E}[\frac{1-kt}{k}X]$ of vertices of $T$ are $0$ and not $w_v-t$ as written above, but this makes no difference since $\sum_{v\in T}(w_v-t)=\sum_{v\in T}0$. The equation~(\ref{eq:expectation_X}) shows $\mathbb{E} X\leq 1$.

Take $k-d$ independent copies of $X$, denoted by $X_1, \dots, X_{k-d}$, and say the corresponding vertices of $\mathcal{H}$ are $v_1, \dots, v_{k-d}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:feige} applied to $k-d$ random variables and $\delta=d\geq 1$, we have 
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:feige_applied}
\mathbb P\Big[\sum_{j=1}^{k-d} X_j< (k-d)+d\Big]\geq \Big(1-\frac{1}{(k-d)+d}\Big)^{k-d}=\Big(1-\frac{1}{k}\Big)^{k-d}.\vspace{-0.2cm}
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.2cm}

The punchline is that whenever the vertices $v_1, \dots, v_{k-d}$ form a hyperedge together with $T$, we have $dt+\sum_{j=1}^{k-d}w_{v_j}\geq 1$ (since $w$ is a fractional cover), and so we have $\sum_{j=1}^{k-d} X_j=\frac{k}{1-kt}\sum_{j=1}^{k-d}(w_{v_j}-t)\geq \frac{k}{1-kt}\big(1-dt-(k-d)t\big)\geq k$. So, whenever $\sum_{j=1}^{k-d} X_j<k$, the set $\{v_{1}, \dots, v_{k-d}\}\cup T$ is not a hyperedge. Hence, (\ref{eq:feige_applied}) implies that there are at least $\big(1-\frac{1}{k}\big)^{k-d}n^{k-d}$ ways to choose an ordered $(k-d)$-tuple of vertices $(v_1, \dots, v_{k-d})$ which does not form a hyperedge with $T$. 

On the other hand, by the assumption on $\delta_d(\mathcal{H})$, there are at least $(k-d)!\delta_d(\mathcal{H})> \big(1-\big(1-\frac{1}{k}\big)^{k-d}\big)n^{k-d}$ ways to choose an ordered $(k-d)$-tuple of vertices $(v_1, \dots, v_{k-d})$ which forms an edge together with $T$. So, in total, there are more than $n^{k-d}$ ways to choose a $(k-d)$-tuple of vertices $(v_1, \dots, v_{k-d})$, a contradiction.

By increasing the random variables, we may assume that $\mathbb{E} X_i=1$ for all $i$.
A standard reduction, originally observed by Feige \cite{F06}, allows us to assume that random variables $X_i$ are supported on at most two values, as shown in Lemma~\ref{lemma:two_values}. For completeness, we present the proof of this statement after the main proof. It will be convenient to define $\lambda=n+\delta$ and $S=\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$. Our goal will be to give a lower bound on $\mathbb P[S<\lambda]$.

We will perform a sequence of \textit{moves}, where in each move we replace some random variable $X_i$ by its lower value, denoted by $u_i$. After such a move, we obtain the sum $S_i=\sum_{j\neq i} X_j + u_i$. We observe that $S_i\leq S$, meaning that $\mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]\geq \mathbb P[S<\lambda]$. Our first lemma shows a lower bound on $\mathbb P[S<\lambda]$ in terms of $\mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]$. 

To state the exact bound, let us introduce two pieces of notation. For a random variable $S$, we denote $r(S)=\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|S<\lambda]$ and $b(S)=\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S|S<\lambda]$. To motivate these two quantities, we recommend the reader to look at the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:lower_bound}. As we will see, the larger $r(S)$ is, the better the lower bound provided by Lemma~\ref{lemma:lower_bound} will be. Therefore, throughout the replacement process, we would like the value of $r(S)$ to increase, motivating the following definition. A move is \textit{safe} if $r(S_i)\geq r(S)$. Then, we have the following lower bound on $\mathbb P[S<\lambda]$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:lower_bound}
For any move $S\mapsto S_i$, we have
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower_bound}
\mathbb P[S<\lambda]\ge \mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]\Big(1-\frac{b(S)-b(S_i)}{r(S)}\Big).
\end{equation}
If the move is safe, we also have $0\leq b(S)-b(S_i)\leq 1$.
\end{lemma}

Our second lemma shows that there is always a safe move available.

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:safe_move}
Unless $S$ is deterministic, there is some $i$ such that replacing $X_i$ by $u_i$ is a safe move.
\end{lemma}

Let us first show how to complete the proof using the above lemmas, and then we will present their proofs. We start with $S_0=S$ and repeatedly make a safe move until all variables are fixed. This produces a sequence of sums $S_0, S_1, S_2, \dots, S_n$, where $S_n$ is deterministic and at most $n<\lambda$. Since every move is safe, we have $r(S_t)\geq r(S)$ for all $t$ and therefore by repeated application of Lemma~\ref{lemma:lower_bound} we get that
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\[\mathbb P[S<\lambda]\ge \mathbb P[S_n<\lambda] \prod_{t=1}^n\left(1-\frac{b(S_{t-1})-b(S_{t})}{r(S_{t-1})}\right)\geq \prod_{t=1}^n\left(1-\frac{b(S_{t-1})-b(S_{t})}{r(S)}\right),\]
since $\mathbb P[S_n<\lambda]=1$ and $r(S_{t-1})\geq r(S)$. 

Let us denote $b=b(S)\in [0, n]$, and observe that $r(S)=\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|S<\lambda]=n+\delta+b(S)-\mathbb{E}[S]=\delta+b$. Moreover, if we denote the summands $b(S_{t-1})-b(S_t)$ by $\Delta_t$, we observe that they telescope $\sum_{t=1}^n\Delta_t=b(S_0)-b(S_n)=b$ and satisfy $\Delta_t\in [0, 1]$. Hence, we have
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\[\mathbb P[S<\lambda]\ge \prod_{t=1}^n\left(1-\frac{\Delta_t}{\delta+b}\right)\ge \left(1-\frac{1}{\delta+b}\right)^{\sum_{t=1}^{n}\Delta_t}=\left(1-\frac{1}{\delta+b}\right)^{b},\]
where the second inequality follows from the fact that $1-\Delta_t/(\delta+b)\geq \big(1-1/(\delta+b)\big)^{\Delta_t}$, by Bernoulli's inequality (which states that for reals $r\in [0, 1]$ and $x>-1$ we have $1+xr\geq (1+x)^r$).

To obtain the final bound, it remains to show that the function $(1-1/(\delta+b))^b$ is minimized when $b=n$. This is the only place where we use the assumption $\delta\geq 1$. By Bernoulli's inequality with exponents at least one (which states that for $r\geq 1$ and $x>-1$ we have $(1+x)^r\geq 1+rx$), we have that 
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\[\Big(1+\frac{1}{n+\delta-1}\Big)^{n/b}\geq 1+\frac{n/b}{n+\delta-1}= 1+\frac{1}{b+(\delta-1)b/n}\geq 1+\frac{1}{b+\delta-1}.\]
\vspace{-0.5cm}

\noindent
Taking reciprocals shows that $1-\frac{1}{b+\delta}\geq \big(1-\frac{1}{n+\delta}\big)^{n/b}$, and so $\mathbb P[S<\lambda]\geq \Big(1-\frac{1}{\delta+b}\Big)^{b}\geq \Big(1-\frac{1}{n+\delta}\Big)^n$. It remains to show the proofs of the two auxiliary lemmas.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:lower_bound}.]
The goal of this proof is to control how the probability $\mathbb P[S<\lambda]$ increases when $X_i$ is replaced by $u_i$. To achieve this, we want an upper bound on the weight of outcomes of $S_i$ which land below $\lambda$, where the corresponding outcome of $S$ did not land below $\lambda$. There is a simple way to bound this using the conditional averages, as follows. 

Consider the event $E_i=\{S_i<\lambda\}$, having probability $\mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]$. This event naturally splits into $E=\{S<\lambda\}$, the outcomes where the original sum was below the threshold, and the newly added outcomes $E_i\backslash E$ where we have $S_i<\lambda$ and $S>\lambda$. The key point is that every new outcome raises the expectation of $S$ on $E_i$ significantly. So, an upper bound on $\mathbb{E}[S|E_i]$ can give us an upper bound on the weight of the outcomes in $E_i\backslash E$. Putting this into equations, we have
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:increase_in_expectation}
\mathbb{E}[S|E_i]\geq \frac{\mathbb P[E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\mathbb{E}[S|E]+\frac{\mathbb P[E_i\backslash E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\lambda=\mathbb{E}[S|E]+\frac{\mathbb P[E_i\backslash E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\big(\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|E]\big).
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.4cm}

\noindent
This argument clarifies why $r(S)=\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|E]$ is the correct quantity to consider. Namely, on every outcome in $E_i\backslash E$, the value of $S$ is by at least $\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|E]$ larger than the previous expectation of $S$, namely $\mathbb{E}[S|E]$. 

Inequality~(\ref{eq:lower_bound}) follows by rearranging (\ref{eq:increase_in_expectation}) to obtain a lower bound on $\mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]=\mathbb P[E_i]$. From (\ref{eq:increase_in_expectation}), we have 

\begin{equation}\label{eq:rearrangement}
    \frac{\mathbb P[E_i\backslash E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\big(\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|E]\big)\leq \mathbb{E}[S|E_i]-\mathbb{E}[S|E]=\mathbb{E}[S_i|E_i]-\mathbb{E}[S|E]+\mathbb{E}[(X_i-u_i)|E_i],
\end{equation}
\vspace{-0.4cm}

\noindent
where we used the linearity of expectation in the last step, as $S=S_i+(X_i-u_i)$. Since $X_i$ and $E_i$ are independent, the right hand side can be conveniently rearranged as $\mathbb{E}[S_i|E_i]-\mathbb{E}[S|E]+\mathbb{E}[X_i-u_i]=\mathbb{E}[S_i|E_i]-\mathbb{E}[S|E]+\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S_i]=b(S)-b(S_i)$. Hence, from (\ref{eq:rearrangement}), we get that 
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{equation*}
   \frac{\mathbb P[E_i\backslash E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}= 1-\frac{\mathbb P[E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\geq \frac{b(S)-b(S_i)}{r(S)}, \quad \text{ and so } \quad \frac{\mathbb P[E]}{\mathbb P[E_i]}\geq 1-\frac{b(S)-b(S_i)}{r(S)}.
\end{equation*}
from where the final inequality (\ref{eq:lower_bound}) directly follows.

Since $S_i\le S$, we have $\mathbb P[S_i<\lambda]\ge \mathbb P[S<\lambda]$, and \eqref{eq:lower_bound} therefore implies $b(S)-b(S_i)\ge 0$. If the move is also safe, then $b(S)-b(S_i)=r(S)-r(S_i)+\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S_i]\le \mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S_i]=\mathbb{E}[X_i-u_i]=1-u_i\leq 1$ (since $X_i\geq 0$ always implies $u_i\geq 0$).
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:safe_move}.]
In this proof, we will show that replacing $X_i$ by $u_i$ is a safe move precisely when $X_i$ is positively correlated with the variable $S$ conditioned on $S<\lambda$. This makes sense: removing such a variable $X_i$ certainly decreases the expectation $\mathbb{E}[S|S<\lambda]$, and consequently increases the difference $r(S)=\lambda-\mathbb{E}[S|S<\lambda]$. We now give the details.

The key observation is that for every random variable $X_i$ appearing in $S$ we have $\operatorname{Cov}(S, X_i|S<\lambda)=(v_i-u_i)\mathbb P[X_i=u_i|S<\lambda](r(S_i)-r(S))$, where $u_i<v_i$ is the support of $X_i$.

Once we prove this, we simply write \vspace{-0.1cm}\[0\leq \operatorname{Var}(S|S<\lambda)=\sum_{i=1}^n \operatorname{Cov}(S, X_i|S<\lambda)=\sum_{i=1}^n (v_i-u_i)\mathbb P[X_i=u_i|S<\lambda](r(S_i)-r(S)),\] where the sums run over the remaining random summands. Since the coefficients $(v_i-u_i)\mathbb P[X_i=u_i|S<\lambda]$ are positive, this shows that for some $i$ we have $r(S_i)\geq r(S)$, ultimately showing there is a safe move.

Let us now prove the key observation. We can write $X_i=u_i+(v_i-u_i)B$ for a Bernoulli variable $B$, and so $\operatorname{Cov}(S, X_i|S<\lambda)=(v_i-u_i)\operatorname{Cov}(S, B|S<\lambda)$. The latter covariance can be computed as follows
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{Cov}\!\big(S, B\big|S<\lambda\big)\!=\!-\!\operatorname{Cov}\!\big(S, 1\!-\!B\big| S\!<\!\lambda\big)\!&=\!\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]\mathbb{E}\big[1\!-\!B\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]\!-\!\mathbb{E}\big[S(1-B)\big|S<\lambda\big]\\
&=\!\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]\mathbb P\big[B\!=\!0\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]\!-\!\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|B\!=\!0\text{ and }S\!<\!\lambda\big]\mathbb P\big[B\!=\!0\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]
\end{align*}
When $B=0$ we have $X_i=u_i$ and $S=S_i$. This means that 
$\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|B\!=\!0\text{ and }S\!<\!\lambda\big]=\mathbb{E}[S_i|S_i<\lambda]$ and therefore
$\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|S\!<\!\lambda\big]\!-\!\mathbb{E}\big[S\big|B\!=\!0\text{ and }S\!<\!\lambda\big]=r(S_i)-r(S)$.
Putting all of the above steps together, we get \[\operatorname{Cov}(S, X_i|S<\lambda)=(v_i-u_i)\operatorname{Cov}(S, B|S<\lambda)= (v_i-u_i)\mathbb P[X_i=u_i|S<\lambda](r(S_i)-r(S)).\qedhere\]
\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:two_values}
Let $X_i$ be a random variable whose support includes at least three different values. Then, $X_i$ can be replaced by another random variable whose support includes at most two of the values from the support of $X_i$ and has the same mean. Moreover, $\mathbb P[\sum_{j=1}^n X_j<n+\delta]$ does not increase through this replacement.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $T=\sum_{j\ne i}X_j$. By standard approximation arguments, we may assume $X_i$ has finite support, say $v_1, \dots, v_m$. Let us denote $p_k=\mathbb P[X_i=v_k]$ and $q_k=\mathbb P[T+v_k<n+\delta]$. By the law of total probability, we have 
\[\mathbb P\Big[\sum_{j=1}^n X_j<n+\delta\Big]=\sum_{k=1}^m \mathbb P[X_i=v_k]\mathbb P\big[T+v_k<n+\delta\big]=\sum_{k=1}^m p_kq_k.\]
Hence, our goal is to minimize the linear program $\sum_{k=1}^m p_kq_k$ over the variables $p_1, \dots, p_m$, while preserving $\mathbb{E} X_i=1$. This means that our program is subject to constraints $\sum_{k=1}^m p_k=1$ and $\sum_{k=1}^m p_kv_k=1$. The program is feasible (since we already know $X_i$ satisfies the constraints), and since it has two constraints, its optimal solution can have at most two nonzero values among $p_1, \dots, p_m$, meaning that the support of $X_i$ can be reduced to only two values without increasing $\mathbb P[\sum_{j=1}^n X_j<n+\delta]$.\hspace{5cm}$\square$
\end{proof}
